{"kind":"assert","text":"Ruling on request 84908e43, identifiers (c+d): the existing lookup\nstays. Retained interpretation is not held-only.\n\nAt main, `ReceiverReads.#interpret` derives the receiver's label again\nat each use. In the legacy mode it reads nothing. In the partition\nmode it calls `proof.source` under the budget and the deadline of the\noperation, and a failed or late lookup gives the delivery its retry\nwith `dependency-unavailable`. The head keeps that call, that budget\nand that answer. A held-only rule would leave a result with no route\nto its label after a cold start, and main has no such stop.\n\n1. The lookup gives the receiver's label and nothing else. The\n   retained use keeps its stored producer, its recorded kind and its\n   bytes, as 74d0455f says.\n2. The frames of the lookup never become a birth, a CheckedSequence or\n   a Fetched for the retained use.\n3. This corrects the last sentence of item 1e of a94bfa4d. It said\n   that nothing is fetched after a restart. The rule is: the head adds\n   no read for a retained use.\n\nThe general rule for the cutover, so that the next case needs no\nquestion: the head adds no read and removes no read. Where main\nreads, the head reads the same thing under the same budget. A place\nwhere the new types make an existing read impossible is a question\nfor me.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}